{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices, especially during downtrends. Apply a downward level shift (roughly 1–3 EUR in the first week, widening to ~4–8 EUR beyond ~10 trading days when trend is negative).",
    "Downtrend underreaction / lag: after a regime change lower, the model projects stabilization or rebound too early. Add a trend-following correction: if the last 10–15 days slope is negative, subtract an extra amount proportional to the recent slope (i.e., enforce continued drift down rather than flat/up).",
    "Excessive mean-reversion: the model repeatedly pulls forecasts back toward ~70–74 even when realized continues to grind lower into the low/mid-60s. Reduce mean-reversion strength when price is below its 20–30d moving average and MA slope is negative.",
    "Horizon error grows with time: near-term (days 1–5) errors are modest, but medium/long horizon (days 10–30) errors become large and mostly one-sided (too high). Use horizon-dependent damping: progressively compress the forecast path downward with horizon (e.g., linear ramp of additional negative adjustment).",
    "Overshoot after short-term upticks: when the last few observations bounce (e.g., 70→73), the model extrapolates a stronger rebound than realized. Cap rebound magnitude: if a 3–5 day rally occurs within a broader 20d downtrend, scale up-moves in the forecast by ~0.5x and re-anchor to the downtrend channel."
  ]
}
